  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nThe forecast period starts with a few high clouds moving across \nnorthern Nebraska and southwest flow over the Great Plains. For the \nrest of today, expect high temperatures to reach the 60s for most \nareas. Heading into this evening, a shortwave trough drops into the \nregion from the Wyoming Big Horns. The system will not have much \nmoisture with it as it reaches us, so conditions should remain dry \nthrough this evening and into the overnight hours. \n\nTomorrow, the shortwave continues to push to the east and low \npressure develops. There will be a decent gradient over the CWA, \nwhich will allow for some stronger winds through the day. Northwest \nwinds of 15-25mph are expected with gusts meeting or exceeding 35mph \nin some locations. Cold air advects into the region Wednesday into \nThursday. Wednesday highs will top out in the mid-50s in northern \nNebraska and the low to mid-60s for areas along and south of the I-\n80 corridor. Overnight lows will fall into the low to mid-30s for \nareas along and north of I-80, while areas south of the interstate \nwill range from the mid to upper 30s. \n\nThe colder air takes hold Thursday, with afternoon highs mainly in \nthe 40s, with a few low 50s possible along the Kansas/Nebraska \nborder.\n\nThursday night/Friday morning, light winds, mostly clear skies, and \nradiational cooling will allow temperatures to plummet. The current \nforecast has widespread lows in the 20s, with low to mid-20s \nexpected for areas along and north of a line from Norfolk to \nTekamah. Those with agricultural interests will want to monitor this \nclosely for potential impacts. Also of note for Friday morning is \nthe low for Omaha. Omaha has not yet fallen below freezing for the \nseason, and with the current forecast putting Omaha around a low of \n27 degrees, this could potentially tie the record for the latest \nfreeze recorded in Omaha.",
